public with sharing class CasePriorityHandler {

    public static void handleBeforeInsert(List<Case> newCases) {
        // Collect the related Account IDs first so we can query in bulk.
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case caseRecord : newCases) {
            if (caseRecord.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(caseRecord.AccountId);
            }
        }

        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // One query for all Accounts, regardless of batch size.
        Map<Id, Account> accountsById = new Map<Id, Account>(
            [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds]
        );

        for (Case caseRecord : newCases) {
            if (caseRecord.AccountId == null) {
                continue;
            }

            Account account = accountsById.get(caseRecord.AccountId);
            if (account != null && account.Rating == 'Hot') {
                caseRecord.Priority = 'High';
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**Deviation — deliberate and required:** I am not doing a synchronous, one-callout-per-account callout from the trigger.

1. A synchronous callout from a trigger fails with “uncommitted work pending” and would hold the save transaction open; the record must commit before the ERP can rely on its Id. An asynchronous `Queueable` with `Database.AllowsCallouts` is the supported pattern.
2. One callout per account breaks in bulk: a 200-account batch would need 200 callouts, exceeding the 100-callout-per-transaction limit. The API contract explicitly accepts an array of 1–200, so I send one request per trigger batch (chunked at 200). Traceability is preserved — every array element carries its own 18-character `salesforceId`.

