**Change explained:** The requested one-job-per-case loop is not bulk-safe. Salesforce allows at most 50 `System.enqueueJob` calls per transaction (and only 1 from an asynchronous transaction), so closing 200 cases would throw a `LimitException` and roll back the whole update. I therefore changed the trigger to collect all newly closed Case Ids and enqueue **one** `CaseSurveyJob` carrying the full `List<Id>`. Per-case isolation is preserved inside the job: it re-queries the cases (fresh `CaseNumber`/`ContactId`, and re-checks `IsClosed` in case a case was reopened before the job ran), builds all Tasks, and inserts with `allOrNone=false`, logging individual failures so one bad Task cannot block the others. This uses 1 queueable job, 1 SOQL query and 1 DML statement for up to 200 cases, respects sharing/user-mode security, and keeps the single-Id constructor so a case can still be submitted individually if ever needed.

