/**
 * Sends renewal reminder emails to opportunity owners.
 *
 * Why the requested one-sendEmail-call-per-opportunity approach is NOT used:
 * Apex permits only 10 Messaging.sendEmail invocations per transaction.
 * With up to 200 opportunities, the eleventh in-loop call would throw an
 * uncatchable System.LimitException and terminate the transaction, preventing
 * the remaining reminders from being sent — the opposite of the intended
 * fault isolation.
 *
 * Instead, all messages are built first and submitted in a single bulk
 * Messaging.sendEmail(messages, false) call. The false allOrNothing argument
 * provides per-message fault isolation: an individual failure is reported in
 * that message's Messaging.SendEmailResult, while the other messages are
 * still sent. Counting the successful results therefore preserves the
 * requested behaviour without exceeding governor limits.
 *
 * Sending to an internal User via setTargetObjectId, as requested, does not
 * consume the org's daily limit for emails to external addresses.
 */
public with sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // Remove nulls and duplicates so an owner is not reminded twice
        // about the same opportunity in a single run.
        Set<Id> uniqueOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id opportunityId : opportunityIds) {
            if (opportunityId != null) {
                uniqueOpportunityIds.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }
        if (uniqueOpportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        List<Opportunity> opportunities = [
            SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :uniqueOpportunityIds
            WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED
        ];

        List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> messages =
            new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>();

        for (Opportunity opp : opportunities) {
            if (opp.OwnerId == null) {
                continue;
            }

            String closeDateText = opp.CloseDate == null
                ? 'not specified'
                : opp.CloseDate.format();

            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage message =
                new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
            message.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
            message.setSaveAsActivity(false);
            message.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);
            message.setPlainTextBody(
                'Hello,\n\n' +
                'The opportunity "' + opp.Name + '" is due for renewal.\n' +
                'Close date: ' + closeDateText + '\n\n' +
                'Please review the opportunity and follow up as appropriate.'
            );
            messages.add(message);
        }

        if (messages.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // One bulk invocation only; allOrNothing = false so that one failed
        // email does not prevent the others from being sent.
        List<Messaging.SendEmailResult> results =
            Messaging.sendEmail(messages, false);

        Integer sentCount = 0;
        for (Messaging.SendEmailResult result : results) {
            if (result.isSuccess()) {
                sentCount++;
            }
        }
        return sentCount;
    }
}
```

**Why I changed your approach:** Loading `[SELECT ... FROM Contact]` unfiltered would return ~2.3 million rows for every 200-account batch. That exceeds the 50,000-row SOQL governor limit (`Too many query rows: 50001`), as well as heap/CPU limits, so it would fail before any logic ran — regardless of being outside a loop. The fix below keeps your intent — exactly one SOQL query, outside all loops, no DML in loops — but makes it selective: first collect only the accounts whose `BillingCountry` actually changed, then query only *their* contacts with `WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds`. This is bulk-safe for batches of 200, returns early with no query/DML when no country changed, and skips contacts already at the new value to avoid needless DML.

